# Header: Apple's Virtualization.framework virtiofs server synthesizes each
# FUSE reply's uid and gid from the caller's request header instead of keeping
# real per-file ownership, and caches those attributes for 1 s on the shared
# guest inode. When another UID touches the same share — kiri-init (uid 0)
# mounting bind sources or walking /proc/kiri-fsnotify for inotify watchers,
# or another container — it overwrites the shared inode's i_uid/i_gid to 0 or
# that container's UID. Concurrent non-root operations then race in
# fuse_permission() / setattr_prepare() and fail with EACCES / EPERM (#796).
# On virtiofs, treat the calling task as the inode's owner/group when checking
# mode bits, DAC capabilities, setattr, sticky directories, and cached stat,
# matching what Apple's virtiofs server already reports to that caller.
